\b\fs28 Advantages of Binary search tree\par

\pard{\pntext\f4\'B7\tab}{\*\pn\pnlvlblt\pnf4\pnindent0{\pntxtb\'B7}}\fi-360\li720\sa200\sl240\slmult1\qj\b0\fs24 Searching an element in the Binary search tree is easy as we always have a hint that which subtree has the desired element.\par
{\pntext\f4\'B7\tab}As compared to array and linked lists, insertion and deletion operations are faster in BST.\par

\pard\sa200\sl240\slmult1\qj\b\fs28 Searching in Binary search tree\par
\b0\fs24 Searching means to find or locate a specific element or node in a data structure. In Binary search tree, searching a node is easy because elements in BST are stored in a specific order. The steps of searching a node in Binary Search tree are listed as follows:\par

\pard 
{\pntext\f0 1.\tab}{\*\pn\pnlvlbody\pnf0\pnindent0\pnstart1\pndec{\pntxta.}}
\fi-360\li720\sa200\sl240\slmult1\qj First, compare the element to be searched with the root element of the tree.\par
{\pntext\f0 2.\tab}If root is matched with the target element, then return the node's location.\par
{\pntext\f0 3.\tab}If it is not matched, then check whether the item is less than the root element, if it is smaller than the root element, then move to the left subtree.\par
{\pntext\f0 4.\tab}If it is larger than the root element, then move to the right subtree.\par
{\pntext\f0 5.\tab}Repeat the above procedure recursively until the match is found.\par
{\pntext\f0 6.\tab}If the element is not found or not present in the tree, then return NULL.\par

