\b\fs28 When the node to be deleted has two children\b0\fs24\par
This case of deleting a node in BST is a bit complex among other two cases. In such a case, the steps to be followed are listed as follows :\par

\pard{\pntext\f4\'B7\tab}{\*\pn\pnlvlblt\pnf4\pnindent0{\pntxtb\'B7}}\fi-360\li720\sa200\sl240\slmult1\qj First, find the inorder successor of the node to be deleted.\par
{\pntext\f4\'B7\tab}After that, replace that node with the inorder successor until the target node is placed at the leaf of tree.\par
{\pntext\f4\'B7\tab}And at last, replace the node with NULL and free up the allocated space.\par

\pard\sa200\sl240\slmult1\qj The inorder successor is required when the right child of the node is not empty. We can obtain the inorder successor by finding the minimum element in the right child of the node.\par
We can see the process of deleting a node with two children from BST in the below image. In the below image, suppose we have to delete node 45 that is the root node, as the node to be deleted has two children, so it will be replaced with its inorder successor. Now, node 45 will be at the leaf of the tree so that it can be deleted easily.\par
